      "completion_tokens": 1060,
      "output": "\n\nWater is a chemical substance with the molecular formula **H₂O**, consisting of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom. It is a vital component of life and one of the most abundant and essential substances on Earth. Here's a breakdown of its key characteristics:\n\n### **Chemical and Physical Properties**\n- **Molecular Structure**: Water molecules are **polar**, meaning they have a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side and a slight negative charge on the oxygen side. This polarity allows water to form **hydrogen bonds** between molecules, giving it unique properties like high surface tension, cohesion (stickiness), and adhesion (ability to stick to other surfaces).\n- **States of Matter**: At standard temperature and pressure, water exists as a **liquid**, but it can also transition into **solid (ice)** when frozen or **gas (water vapor)** when heated. These phase changes are central to Earth's **hydrological cycle**.\n- **Unique Properties**: Water has a **high specific heat capacity**, meaning it absorbs and retains heat efficiently, helping regulate temperatures in organisms and the environment. It also has a **high heat of vaporization**, making it effective for cooling (e.g., sweating in humans).\n\n### **Biological Significance**\n- **Essential for Life**: All known forms of life depend on water. It acts as a **solvent** for biochemical reactions, transports nutrients and waste in organisms, and maintains cell structure.\n- **Cellular Function**: Water makes up 60–90% of the human body and is critical for processes like digestion, circulation, and temperature regulation.\n\n### **Environmental Role**\n- **Hydrological Cycle**: Water cycles through evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and runoff, shaping weather patterns, ecosystems, and geological features (e.g., rivers, lakes, glaciers).\n- **Habitat**: It supports aquatic life and is a key factor in climate regulation, influencing weather and biodiversity.\n\n### **Other Characteristics**\n- **Appearance**: Pure water is **colorless, odorless, and tasteless**. In large quantities, it appears blue due to light absorption and scattering.\n- **Abundance**: About **71% of Earth's surface** is covered by water, though most is saline (ocean water), with only a small fraction being fresh water.\n\nIn summary, water is a simple yet extraordinary molecule whose unique properties make it indispensable for life, environmental stability, and countless natural and industrial processes."
